{"kind":"assert","text":"CDF-2 ruling on ca7b18ce. Part 2 answers yes, so the wider bound holds\nthe cutover deployment.\n\nOne head. Build the total in the CDF-1 head, on\n`builder/cdf1-scope-bytes-cap-20261113`, before its gate. No deployed\nstore holds the row `retained-entry-bytes`. So the head names the row\n`retained-bytes` from the start, and it carries no code that removes\nthe old name. CDF-1 (27cb5f60) keeps its promise, and the head takes\none gate and one review. I retire CDF-2 (b5750e6e) with this ruling\nas the reason.\n\nThe total: build part 3 as you propose.\n- Count the canonical bytes of each new row of `retained_input` and\n  `retained_value`, one time for each key. `forget` subtracts.\n- The constructor fill sums both tables in one transaction.\n- The `retained_birth` association rows stay out of the total.\n- A scope that is past the bound answers as you list.\n- `Store.retainedEntryBytes()` takes the name `retainedBytes()`.\n\na. Yes. An outcome that settles an operation of this scope passes\n   the stop. The stop exempts the inputs that `fits` exempts in a\n   scope without the opt-in (`derive/src/reserve.ts:243`): `timed`,\n   `diagnosis`, `outcome`, a control delivery, and a result delivery\n   whose clause is not `conflict`. A comment at the stop names that\n   line. One test names each input type and fails when the stop and\n   `fits` exempt different types. One shared predicate is an export\n   from derive, so it goes on the consolidation list, not in this\n   head.\n\nb. No ceiling for one rule row in this head. A ceiling changes the\n   inputs that a scope accepts, so replay must agree, and that is\n   kernel work under its own request. The faults note states the\n   bound that holds today: the largest size of one rule row from its\n   parts, the largest count of pending settlements in the shipped\n   platform definitions, and the product, which is the overshoot\n   above `scopeBytes`. Send me the three numbers when you have them.\n\nc. Yes. Request FL-1 (8bc1d2aded6d6ae8ed7daa13da35e509fcce1bd0)\n   holds it. FL-1 does not hold the cutover deployment.\n\nCorrections. The faults note takes your two corrections: ordinary\nacts write `definition` rows, and `text` is a fifth kind. The comment\nof `scopeBytes` in `contract/src/bounds.ts` states that the total\ncovers every retained row in every scope.\n\nWitnesses, each failing at 5339ccc5:\n1. The fault: a member's acts that meet a rule guard add `rule` rows,\n   and at the bound the next one gets `scope-full` with `scopeBytes`\n   and writes nothing.\n2. A new `retained_value` row counts.\n3. A redaction subtracts the bytes of its text.\n4. The fill of an old store gives the sum of both tables.\n\nOrder: CF-1, then this head. The checker reviews it with the Security\nconclusion.","body":{"to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}